    "body": "### Problem\n\nAs the docs [cleary say](https://neovim.io/doc/user/treesitter/#TSQuery%3Adisable_pattern()):\n> TSQuery:disable_pattern({pattern_index}) : [..] once disabled the pattern cannot be re-enabled.\n\nHowever, I ran into an issue that re-setting the same pattern with `treesitter.query.set` did not work.\nHowever, I think I accidently succeeded in re-enabling the said pattern just by appending a fresh comment to the query.\n\nAs a result, I can toggle a pattern on and off using the following technique:\n```lua\nlocal my_pattern_id = nil -- Strangely remains stable after first setup.\nlocal counter = 0 -- Strangely enables toggling.\n\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"ON\", function()\n  local patterns = vim.treesitter.query.get(\"lua\", \"highlights\").info.patterns\n  local latest_pattern_id\n  for i, _ in pairs(patterns) do latest_pattern_id = i end\n  local q = [[;; extends\n    ((identifier) @variable.builtin (#set! priority 500))\n  ]]\n  if my_pattern_id == nil then\n    my_pattern_id = latest_pattern_id + 1\n  else\n    q = q..\"\\n;magic-fresh-comment-\"..counter\n    counter = counter + 1\n  end\n  vim.treesitter.query.set(\"lua\", \"highlights\", q)\n  vim.treesitter.stop(0)\n  vim.treesitter.start(0, \"lua\")\nend)\n\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"OFF\", function()\n  local q = vim.treesitter.query.get(\"lua\", \"highlights\")\n  q.query:disable_pattern(my_pattern_id)\n  vim.treesitter.stop(0)\n  vim.treesitter.start(0, \"lua\")\nend)\n```\n\nIs this really an accident? Or is this something I can safely rely on to toggle my additional custom pattern?\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIf the above behaviour is reliable, then I would be happy that something along the following:\n```lua\nlocal my_new_patterns = vim.treesitter.query.set(\"lua\", \"highlights\", my_extend_query)\nlocal q = vim.treesitter.query.get(\"lua\", \"highlights\").query\nq:disable_patterns(my_new_patterns)\nq:reenable_patterns(my_new_patterns)\n```\n.. be officially supported.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
